 "moments": [
  {
   "a": 2,
   "b": 6,
   "s": 12.66,
   "e": 51.92,
   "score": 60.9,
   "hook": "First of all, I'd like to mention the fact that we received two awards from the ISS\u2026",
   "why": "Strong opening line, clean ending, steady pace (built-in scorer).",
   "start": 12.63,
   "end": 52.57,
   "duration": 39.94,
   "text": "First of all, I'd like to mention the fact that we received two awards from the ISS Research Program. One for the CVB experiment, which is a heat pipe -related research. research, and the second for plant research in which they use biomarkers to find out what genes are expressed when you go into microgravity. We're very proud of that work that our researchers have done. In 2014 Procter & Gamble received an award from the ISS program as well, and their work was looking at colloidal systems and the effect of colloids on commercial product stabilizers.",
   "id": "m1"
  },
  {
   "a": 9,
   "b": 13,
   "s": 71.56,
   "e": 105.8,
   "score": 63.4,
   "hook": "Biofilms are very important to NASA.",
   "why": "Strong opening line, clean ending, steady pace (built-in scorer).",
   "start": 71.54,
   "end": 106.04,
   "duration": 34.5,
   "text": "Biofilms are very important to NASA. This is something that's undesired. We are looking for materials that are resistant to biofilms aboard the International Space station, we put it to use right away, and ultimately for crewed missions to Mars where the astronauts have to remain a long period of time in a very small and closed capsule and the bacteria can grow and this all must be contained. And we feel that this is an important area to help not only people on Earth but also NASA in its long duration missions.",
   "id": "m2"
  },
  {
   "a": 16,
   "b": 19,
   "s": 119.34,
   "e": 158.23,
   "score": 51.6,
   "hook": "Both of these allow us to begin the process of being able to manipulate the particles\u2026",
   "why": "Strong opening line, clean ending, steady pace (built-in scorer).",
   "start": 119.32,
   "end": 158.36,
   "duration": 39.04,
   "text": "Both of these allow us to begin the process of being able to manipulate the particles that our colloidal scientists will be studying as opposed to a randomly ordered system. we can now control the orientation of the particles and begin to look at changing the material properties of this soft matter physics. So to wrap up where we are and what we've done, the LMM was launched in 2009. Since then we have successfully completed 12 experiments and we plan to run 15 to 20 more.",
   "id": "m3"
  }
 ],
